(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["nutrition-class-nutrition-ppt-1-2-review-qs"]={"title":"Nutrition PPT 1-2 Review Qs","category":"Nutrition","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"A calorie is best defined as a unit of what?","choices":["Chemical mass","Heat energy","Electrical energy","Protein density"],"answer":1,"exp":"A calorie is the heat energy required to raise the temperature of water. Per the slide, a kilocalorie (kcal) is the heat energy required to raise the temperature of 1,000 g (1 L) of water by 1 °C; a little-c calorie raises 1 g of water by 1 °C. Food energy is reported in kcal. — Human Nutrition, slides 10 & 22 (“Energy From Food” / “Calories Defined”)"},{"q":"Which macronutrient provides the most calories per gram?","choices":["Fat (lipid)","Carbohydrate","Protein","Water"],"answer":0,"exp":"Fat delivers ~9 kcal/g — more than double carbohydrate or protein at ~4 kcal/g each. Water provides none. — Human Nutrition, slides 15–16 & 47"},{"q":"A food containing 10 grams of fat provides how many calories?","choices":["90 kcal","40 kcal","70 kcal","120 kcal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Fat is 9 kcal/g, so 10 g × 9 = 90 kcal. — Human Nutrition, slide 16 (“Calorie Overview”)"},{"q":"Which is the physiologic drive to eat?","choices":["Appetite","Both equally","Neither","Hunger"],"answer":3,"exp":"Hunger is the physiologic drive — governed by the hypothalamus (the feeding/satiety center). Appetite is the psychologic desire to eat. — Human Nutrition, slide 4 (Hunger – hypothalamus)"},{"q":"What distinguishes macronutrients from micronutrients?","choices":["They provide energy (calories)","They are needed in microgram amounts","They contain no carbon","They cannot be stored"],"answer":0,"exp":"Macronutrients — carbohydrate, protein, and lipid — provide calories and are needed in gram amounts. Micronutrients (vitamins, minerals) yield no energy and are needed in mg/mcg amounts. — Human Nutrition, slide 11"},{"q":"Alcohol provides how many calories per gram?","choices":["4 kcal/g","9 kcal/g","7 kcal/g","0 kcal/g"],"answer":2,"exp":"Alcohol yields ~7 kcal/g — between carbohydrate/protein (4) and fat (9). It supplies energy but is not a nutrient. — Human Nutrition, slide 16 (“Calorie Overview”)"},{"q":"Sugars, starches, and dietary fibers are all classified as which macronutrient?","choices":["Dietary protein","Dietary fat","Carbohydrates","Trace minerals"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sugars and starches are simple/complex carbohydrates, and fiber is a non-digestible complex carbohydrate — all carbohydrates. — Human Nutrition, slide 13"},{"q":"A carbohydrate is classified as “complex” rather than “simple” when it contains:","choices":["Only a single sugar unit","More than two sugar units","Exactly two sugar units","Zero linked sugar units"],"answer":1,"exp":"Simple carbohydrates are monosaccharides (1 unit) and disaccharides (2 units). Anything longer — more than two units — is a complex carbohydrate (starch, glycogen, fiber). — Human Nutrition, slide 13"},{"q":"Which macronutrient supplies the majority of the body's energy?","choices":["Protein sources","Fat reserves","Vitamin intake","Carbohydrates"],"answer":3,"exp":"Carbohydrates supply most of the body's energy and should be 45–65% of intake — the fuel for every cell, especially the brain, nervous system, and red blood cells. — Human Nutrition slide 13; Macro slide 5"},{"q":"Which is a benefit of dietary fiber?","choices":["Raises blood cholesterol","Improves glycemic control","Speeds blood-glucose spikes","Increases colon-cancer risk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Fiber slows glucose release (glycemic control), lowers colon-cancer risk, adds satiety, and soluble fiber lowers cholesterol — the opposite of every distractor. — Macro slide 13 (“Benefits of Fiber”)"},{"q":"Carbohydrate is broken down into glucose and stored in the body as what?","choices":["Starch","Triglycerides","Amylose","Glycogen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Glucose is stored as glycogen — branched glucose synthesized in the liver and stored in liver and muscle for quick availability. — Macro slide 5"},{"q":"Which organ synthesizes glycogen?","choices":["Kidney","Pancreas","Liver","Spleen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Glycogen is synthesized in the liver (and stored in both liver and muscle). — Macro slide 5"},{"q":"Omega-3 fatty acids are associated with which health effect?","choices":["Increased blood clotting","Decreased blood clotting","Increased inflammation","Raised triglycerides"],"answer":1,"exp":"Omega-3s decrease blood clotting and inflammation and may reduce heart attacks (research is mixed), and they lower triglycerides. Exam trap: excess omega-3 can raise hemorrhagic-stroke risk. — Macro slide 55 (“Omega-3 Health-related Effects”)"},{"q":"A monounsaturated fatty acid contains how many double bonds?","choices":["Zero","More than two","One","Two"],"answer":2,"exp":"Mono = one double bond. Zero double bonds = saturated; more than two = polyunsaturated (e.g., omega-3 / omega-6). — Macro slide 51"},{"q":"Amino acids that must be obtained through the diet are classified as:","choices":["Nonessential","Conditional","Essential","Complementary"],"answer":2,"exp":"Essential amino acids cannot be synthesized by the body and must come from the diet — 9 of the 20 amino acids. — Macro slide 72"},{"q":"Which macronutrient produces the greatest feeling of fullness?","choices":["Protein","Carbohydrate","Fat","Alcohol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Protein gives the highest satiety — it's a large molecule that takes time to break down. — Macro slide 83"},{"q":"Which is an appropriate protein source for a vegan?","choices":["Grilled salmon","Scrambled eggs","Soy and legumes","Plain Greek yogurt"],"answer":2,"exp":"A vegan eats only plant foods — so no fish, eggs, or dairy. Soy provides all 9 essential amino acids, and legumes are a staple plant protein. — Macro slides 92 & 74"},{"q":"Which set of features characterizes marasmus?","choices":["Severe weight loss with muscle wasting","Generalized edema with an enlarged fatty liver","Rapid onset with normal weight","Edema with fairly preserved muscle bulk"],"answer":0,"exp":"Marasmus is starvation from insufficient protein AND calories — gradual, severe weight loss with muscle and fat wasting. Edema points to kwashiorkor instead, so any answer with edema is wrong. — Macro slide 88 (notes); kwashiorkor/edema, slides 80–81"},{"q":"Which is an example of negative protein balance?","choices":["Pregnancy","Kidney disease","Athletic training","A healthy adult meeting needs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Negative protein balance = protein loss exceeds intake, e.g., increased protein loss from kidney disease (also fasting, burns, infections). Pregnancy and athletic training are positive balance; a healthy adult meeting needs is equilibrium. — Macro slide 85 (“Protein Balance”)"},{"q":"Which nutrient contributes most to satiety?","choices":["Carbohydrate","Water","Vitamins","Protein"],"answer":3,"exp":"Protein produces the highest feeling of fullness after a meal — the same principle as the earlier satiety question, which is why she asked it twice. — Macro slide 83"},{"q":"Which is the most nutrient-dense choice?","choices":["Frosted shredded wheat","Creamed spinach","Canned peaches","Baked chicken"],"answer":3,"exp":"Baked chicken is lean protein with no added sugar or fat. The frosted cereal and syrup-packed peaches add sugar and the creamed spinach adds fat, all of which dilute nutrient density. This is an application item — minimally processed, higher-nutrient foods win. — Concept — nutrient density; Macro slide 13"},{"q":"Which nutrient makes up the majority of body weight?","choices":["Protein","Fat","Water","Carbohydrate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Water makes up 50–70% of body weight — the single largest component. — Human Nutrition slide 20; Macro slide 132"},{"q":"Which vitamin is water-soluble?","choices":["Vitamin A","Vitamin D","Vitamin K","Vitamin C"],"answer":3,"exp":"The fat-soluble vitamins are A, D, E, and K — everything else (the B vitamins and C) is water-soluble. — Human Nutrition slide 18"},{"q":"Which term describes a carbon-containing compound needed in small amounts for normal growth, function, and maintenance?","choices":["Vitamin","Mineral","Electrolyte","Fatty acid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Vitamins are carbon-containing (organic) compounds needed in small amounts. The trap is “mineral” — minerals are inorganic and contain no carbon. — Macro slide 99; contrast Human Nutrition slide 19 (minerals)"},{"q":"Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of death in North America. Which diet is recommended to help reduce it?","choices":["Ketogenic diet","DASH diet","Carnivore diet","Low-fiber diet"],"answer":1,"exp":"The DASH diet — high in calcium, potassium, and magnesium, low in fat and sodium, rich in fruits and vegetables — targets blood pressure and cardiovascular risk. (Mediterranean also works but wasn't an answer choice.) — Macro slide 68 (CVD = leading cause) & slide 158 (DASH)"}]};
